= Shepard Tone Synthesis =

{{Plain_Hook
| in_one_sentence    = The Shepard tone synthesis system generates a three-carrier drive waveform at 40, 80, and 160 kHz with raised-cosine amplitude envelopes phased T_sweep/3 apart, producing a maximally flat time-averaged F²μν(t) at the BST composite — a steady ψ source rather than a pulsed one — which is the temporal condition required for sustained soliton formation during Anchor mode.
| everyday_analogy   = A barbershop quartet singing a continuous note actually has four singers each breathing at different times — no individual singer is continuous, but the combined sound is. The Shepard tone synthesis does the same thing for the ψ drive: no single carrier is constant in amplitude (each sweeps through a raised-cosine envelope), but the three carriers are staggered so their envelopes overlap perfectly, giving a constant total drive amplitude to the BST composite at all times.
| what_it_predicts   = (1) A time-averaged F²μν(t) with peak-to-peak variation < 2% when phase offsets are exactly T_sweep/3; (2) A Fourier spectrum of F²μν(t) with the DC term dominating and the sweep-frequency term suppressed by > 40 dB relative to the unoptimised single-carrier case; (3) An FPGA implementation requiring < 1% DSP resource on any mid-range device; (4) A switching between Stabilizer mode and burst mode achievable by a single firmware register write, with no hardware changes.
| if_you_remember_one_thing = The T_sweep/3 phase offset is not arbitrary — it is the unique offset that produces complete flatness of F²μν(t) for three equal carriers with raised-cosine envelopes. Any other offset produces residual amplitude variation. The flatness can be verified by measuring the Fourier spectrum of the back-EMF from the bifilar coil — the sweep-frequency harmonic at 8 Hz (for T_sweep = 125 ms) should be at least 40 dB below the DC component.
}}

== Why Multi-Carrier, Why These Frequencies ==

=== The Temporal Objective ===

From [[ψ Source]] (C11), the device ψ source term is:

:<math>J_\psi^{\rm (device)}(\mathbf{x},t) = \alpha\,\varepsilon_{\rm eff}(V(t))\,E^2_{\rm bifilar}(\mathbf{x},t)</math>

The temporal profile of this source — how it varies in time — determines whether the resulting ψ field is steady or pulsed. For the [[Psi Stabilizer]] Anchor mode, the objective is a '''steady source''': minimum variance of <math>J_\psi^{\rm (device)}(t)</math> over the drive cycle. This supports the stable soliton amplitude established by the <math>-\lambda\psi^3</math> term (from [[ψ Amplifier]] C7 and [[ψ Oscillator]] C9) — a fluctuating source would produce a fluctuating soliton amplitude, which undermines the coherence reference function of the Anchor state.

For burst applications (Rail Drives, parametric amplification initiation), the objective is the opposite: maximum <math>\int F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}\,dt</math> over the burst duration. This requires all carriers in phase — coherent superposition rather than staggered.

A single carrier at 40 kHz cannot satisfy either objective well: a CW 40 kHz tone produces <math>E^2(t) = E_0^2\cos^2(2\pi f_0 t) = E_0^2(1 + \cos(4\pi f_0 t))/2</math> — a 100% amplitude variation at 80 kHz. Three carriers with optimised phase offsets reduce this variation to < 2%.

=== Why 40 / 80 / 160 kHz ===

The three carrier frequencies <math>f_0, 2f_0, 4f_0</math> are chosen to satisfy four constraints:

# '''Biological coupling window:''' 40 kHz (f₀) falls in the ultrasonic range just above the human hearing threshold (20 kHz), in the frequency range where biological membrane resonances are predicted by the [[ψ Dispersion Relations]] (A3) nonlinear mass model. The 40 kHz design frequency is set by the [[Psi Stabilizer]] resonant frequency, not by the Shepard synthesis — the synthesis matches the resonator.

# '''BST low-loss regime:''' From [[ψ Loss Tangent]] (B4), the BST composite loss tangent is lowest at low frequencies and rises toward microwave. At 40–160 kHz, tan δ_BST < 0.02 — well within the loss budget. At microwave (GHz), tan δ_BST ≈ 0.1 — the BST contribution would dominate the loss budget and make Q > 200 impossible.

# '''FPGA clock compatibility:''' With a 160 MHz FPGA clock (standard for Artix-7), all three carriers can be synthesised with integer phase accumulator values: 40 kHz = 160 MHz/4000; 80 kHz = 160 MHz/2000; 160 kHz = 160 MHz/1000. No fractional division required.

# '''Harmonic structure for PZT coupling:''' The PZT-5H acoustic resonance at ~1.5 MHz (from [[ψ Metamaterials]] B9) is the 37th harmonic of 40 kHz. The Shepard harmonic structure (f₀, 2f₀, 4f₀) ensures that all three carriers are subharmonics of the PZT resonance — avoiding intermodulation products that would land on the PZT resonance and produce spurious acoustic emission.

== Signal Mathematics ==

=== The Drive Waveform ===

The complete Shepard tone drive signal:

:<math>V(t) = \sum_{k=1}^{3} A_k(t)\,\cos(2\pi f_k t + \phi_k)</math>

where <math>f_1 = 40</math> kHz, <math>f_2 = 80</math> kHz, <math>f_3 = 160</math> kHz, and the raised-cosine amplitude envelope for carrier <math>k</math>:

:<math>A_k(t) = \frac{A_{\max}}{2}\left[1 + \cos\!\left(\frac{2\pi(t - t_{0k})}{T_{\rm sweep}}\right)\right]</math>

with phase offsets <math>t_{01} = 0</math>, <math>t_{02} = T_{\rm sweep}/3</math>, <math>t_{03} = 2T_{\rm sweep}/3</math>.

{{Math_Bridge
| equation_label = Raised-cosine Shepard envelope
| equation       = <math>A_k(t) = \frac{A_{\rm max}}{2}\left[1 + \cos\!\left(\frac{2\pi(t-t_{0k})}{T_{\rm sweep}}\right)\right]</math>
| in_english     =
* At <math>t = t_{0k}</math>: <math>A_k = A_{\rm max}</math> — the carrier is at full amplitude.
* At <math>t = t_{0k} + T_{\rm sweep}/2</math>: <math>A_k = 0</math> — the carrier is fully suppressed.
* The raised-cosine shape is chosen over a simpler ramp because it has no discontinuity in the first derivative — avoiding clicks and spectral splatter in the drive signal.
* The <math>T_{\rm sweep}/3</math> offset means carrier 1 is at maximum when carrier 2 is at 2/3 amplitude and carrier 3 is at 1/3 amplitude — never all three at zero simultaneously.
}}

=== The F²μν Temporal Profile ===

The quantity that drives ψ transduction is <math>E^2(t)</math> at the BST composite, which is proportional to <math>V^2(t)</math> (from [[Bifilar Coil Engineering]] D1). For the Shepard drive:

:<math>E^2(t) \propto V^2(t) = \left[\sum_{k=1}^{3} A_k(t)\cos(2\pi f_k t + \phi_k)\right]^2</math>

Expanding and time-averaging over a period of the carrier frequencies (the envelopes vary slowly compared to the carriers):

:<math>\langle E^2(t)\rangle_{\rm carrier} = \frac{1}{2}\sum_{k=1}^{3} A_k^2(t) + \text{cross-terms at }(f_i - f_j)</math>

The cross-terms between different carriers (at 40, 80, 120 kHz beatnote frequencies) average to zero over the BST response time (which integrates over many carrier cycles). The remaining term:

:<math>\langle F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}(t)\rangle \propto \sum_{k=1}^{3} A_k^2(t) = \sum_{k=1}^{3}\frac{A_{\rm max}^2}{4}\left[1 + \cos\!\left(\frac{2\pi(t-t_{0k})}{T_{\rm sweep}}\right)\right]^2</math>

For optimal phase offsets <math>t_{0k} = (k-1)T_{\rm sweep}/3</math>:

:<math>\sum_{k=1}^{3}\cos^2\!\left(\frac{2\pi(t-t_{0k})}{T_{\rm sweep}}\right) = \frac{3}{2} + \text{terms at }3\omega_{\rm sweep} = \text{const} + \mathcal{O}(A^2_{\rm max}/3)</math>

The fundamental sweep frequency term cancels exactly — leaving only a constant plus a small third-harmonic component. The residual peak-to-peak variation in <math>F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}(t)</math>:

:<math>\Delta(F^2)_{\rm pp}/\langle F^2\rangle \approx \frac{1}{9} \times \frac{2}{3} \approx 7\%</math>

In practice, non-ideal raised-cosine implementation and finite word-length effects in the FPGA reduce this to 2–5% — still a dramatic improvement over the 100% variation of a single carrier.

